WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.240
How do you find the genes for a death, when you do not know which proteins are

2
00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:08.240
involved?

3
00:00:08.683 --> 00:00:12.703
The worm C. elegans is about a millimetre long, and it is transparent.

4
00:00:13.817 --> 00:00:18.797
With Nomarski optics, its nuclei are visible in the living animal, without any

5
00:00:18.797 --> 00:00:19.797
staining.

6
00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:24.700
Researchers followed its cells as they divided, and drew the full family tree.

7
00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:27.520
Some branches end in a death.

8
00:00:28.340 --> 00:00:30.540
On the chart, each death is marked with an X.

9
00:00:31.450 --> 00:00:35.910
The same identified cells die in every animal, at the same place in the tree.

10
00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:43.680
Counting both lineages, 131 of the hermaphrodite's 1,090 somatic cells die during

11
00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:44.680
development.

12
00:00:45.233 --> 00:00:49.033
A death that happens to a named cell, on schedule, is a phenotype.

13
00:00:49.673 --> 00:00:51.633
And a phenotype can be screened for.

14
00:00:52.617 --> 00:00:56.097
Horvitz's laboratory started from a mutant called ced-1.

15
00:00:56.997 --> 00:00:59.637
In it, corpses are not engulfed, so they stay visible.

16
00:01:00.667 --> 00:01:04.367
They searched for new mutations in which the corpses never appeared.

17
00:01:05.417 --> 00:01:09.597
In 1986 they reported two genes, ced-3 and ced-4.

18
00:01:10.550 --> 00:01:13.710
In strong mutants, the cells that should have died lived.

19
00:01:14.570 --> 00:01:16.050
Some took on features of neurons.

20
00:01:17.133 --> 00:01:19.493
Then came a gene that works the other way.

21
00:01:20.253 --> 00:01:21.253
It is called ced-9.

22
00:01:22.233 --> 00:01:26.093
A gain-of-function mutation in ced-9 kept doomed cells alive.

23
00:01:27.133 --> 00:01:30.013
Loss of ced-9 killed cells that should have lived.

24
00:01:30.013 --> 00:01:33.673
With severe loss of ced-9, the worms die as embryos.

25
00:01:34.867 --> 00:01:37.787
Put the results in a table, one genotype per row.

26
00:01:38.707 --> 00:01:41.287
Losing ced-3 or ced-4 saves doomed cells.

27
00:01:42.187 --> 00:01:44.147
Losing ced-9 kills extra cells.

28
00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:47.460
Now remove both ced-9 and ced-3.

29
00:01:48.260 --> 00:01:49.740
Do the extra deaths still happen?

30
00:01:56.133 --> 00:01:59.813
No. Few cells die, and the embryos survive.

31
00:02:00.833 --> 00:02:04.373
Removing ced-4 instead of ced-3 gives the same result.

32
00:02:05.417 --> 00:02:09.897
If removing the killer cancels the effect of removing the brake, the brake works

33
00:02:09.897 --> 00:02:10.897
through the killer.

34
00:02:11.567 --> 00:02:14.407
So, in genetic order, CED-9 acts upstream.

35
00:02:15.367 --> 00:02:16.707
It is a brake on the two killers.

36
00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:21.280
Genetic order does not, by itself, show which proteins touch.

37
00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:24.420
One more gene sits above the brake.

38
00:02:25.240 --> 00:02:26.540
It is called EGL-1.

39
00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:31.880
EGL-1 carries only a short stretch, called a BH3 domain.

40
00:02:32.933 --> 00:02:35.413
It binds CED-9 and releases the brake.

41
00:02:36.417 --> 00:02:39.157
It is needed for most somatic cell deaths in the worm.

42
00:02:40.057 --> 00:02:41.617
Without it, most doomed cells live.

43
00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:44.900
So the worm's core pathway has one brake.

44
00:02:45.860 --> 00:02:49.260
CED-9 holds back the killers, and EGL-1 releases it.
